## The temple that kept everything

Most Egyptian temples ask you to imagine what's missing: the roof, the doors, the darkness of the inner rooms. Edfu doesn't. Built by the Ptolemies over almost two centuries, from 237 to 57 BC, the Temple of Horus still stands from its great pylon to its last chamber. Walk in through the gateway and you are following the route the priests walked, from bright courtyard to dim columned halls, until the sanctuary closes around you in near silence.

## The falcon at the door

Horus was the falcon god, the son of Isis and Osiris, and the protector of the king. At Edfu he is everywhere, but nowhere more than in the granite falcon that stands at the doors of the hall, wearing the double crown of Upper and Lower Egypt. Further in, the sanctuary holds a polished granite shrine and a replica of the sacred boat in which the god's statue travelled in procession.

> Most temples make you imagine the roof. Edfu still has it.

## A story you can walk along

Between the temple and its outer wall runs a long corridor, open to the sky, carved from end to end with the myth of Horus avenging his father against Seth. Scene follows scene for the whole length of the wall, and it is often a quiet place to stand. On the Original day, your concierge brings you there and lets you take it in; on the Signature day, a licensed Egyptologist can join you to read it scene by scene.

## Left to us

Edfu is about two hours south of Luxor. When to arrive and how to make the day flow is left to your concierge, with local knowledge and our craft. It's easiest as the centre of our Temple Road day between Luxor and Aswan, with Esna and Kom Ombo on the same road, or as a stop on your way south. Either way there's a €150 road supplement for the longer drive, and we take care of everything else.
